BEGIN;

-- Alinea 32 `displayText` con el `name` de la fila a la que enlazan, en
-- 25 documentos editoriales.
--
-- Salio del barrido de ortografia: al comprobar la correccion de los cuatro
-- nombres del bloque C descubri que **ya habia 101 `displayText` que no
-- coincidian con su destino**, ninguno causado por ese cambio.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- NO TODOS ERAN ERRORES, Y POR ESO SOLO SE TOCAN 32
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- Un `displayText` no tiene por que ser igual al `name`. Escribir "Alexandra"
-- dentro de una frase, enlazando a la ficha "Alexandra Queen", es correcto: el
-- texto corre mejor y el enlace lleva al sitio bueno. Lo mismo con "Juan Luis
-- Guerra" enlazando a "Juan Luis Guerra 4.40", o "Hector Acosta" a la ficha que
-- lleva el apodo entre comillas.
--
-- **56 de los 101 son formas cortas deliberadas de esas. No se tocan.**
--
-- Se arreglan las tres clases donde el texto ES el mismo y solo esta mal
-- escrito:
--
--   ESPACIOS SOBRANTES (8): "Fernando Villalona  " con dos espacios al final,
--     " Wilfrido Vargas" con uno al principio. Tambien Sergio Vargas, Victor
--     Victor y Wason Brazoban.
--
--   ACENTOS PERDIDOS (4): "BS El Ideologo" por "BS El Ideologo" con tilde, y
--     "Rubby Perez" por "Rubby Perez" con tilde.
--
--   MAYUSCULAS Y/O ACENTOS (20): "Melymel" por "MelyMel", "Michel El Buenon"
--     por "Michel el Buenon", "Milly, Jocelyn y los Vecinos" por "...y Los
--     Vecinos", "Yailin la Mas Viral" por "Yailin La Mas Viral", y "Fortuna La
--     Super F" por "Fortuna la Super F", que perdia la tilde y cambiaba dos
--     mayusculas a la vez.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- LOS 13 QUE QUEDAN, Y POR QUE NO SE TOCAN
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- Son decisiones de prosa o problemas de otra naturaleza:
--
--   "El Lapiz Conciente", "El Poeta Callejero", "La Rokabanda" -- el redactor
--     anadio el articulo dentro de la frase. Es correcto en espanol.
--
--   "Secreto El Famoso Biberon" -- el `name` lleva comillas tipograficas y el
--     texto corrido no. Quitarlas dentro de una frase es defendible.
--
--   "Pochy Familia" enlazando a la ficha "Pochy y su Cocoband" -- **esto no es
--     ortografia: es la confusion entre la persona y su orquesta**, que ya
--     estaba senalada como el caso mas urgente de la auditoria de separacion.
--     Arreglarlo aqui taparia el sintoma.
--
-- PARA REVERTIR: supabase/rollback/20260908009700_revert_align_display_text.sql
--
-- Aplicado directamente por DATABASE_URL. No corrio ninguna funcion de Vercel
-- y no se revalido nada.

-- la-insuperable [en]: 1
--   "Yailin la Más Viral" -> "Yailin La Más Viral"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"La Insuperable, born Indhira Ircania Luna Guzmán, is a Dominican singer and songwriter. She was raised in Cristo Rey, she has been at the front of the genre for fifteen years, and for most of that time she was the only woman there.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cero Gogas","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"She did not set out to record. At an event at the end of 2007 somebody heard her, told her that her image and her carriage were too strong to waste, and pushed her into a studio. Her own objection was that she did not like her voice.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cero Gogas came out in 2011, a mambo written by her, and it moved the day it appeared. She has written her own material since.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Que Me Den Banda","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The singles run in an unbroken line: Contigo Quiero Estar, Ojos Que No Ven Corazón Que No Siente, Nota Jamaiquina and Siempre Regia Nunca Camu in 2014, Escondió, No Me Niegue, La Que Ta Buena Soy Yo, then Que Me Den Banda and Cuál Es La Vaina in 2016, Me Subo Arriba in 2017, Cama Vacía in 2018, and Salao, Yo Soy Esa, Su Favorita and Tengo Derecho in 2019.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The subject is nearly always the same and it is worth naming: money of her own, an answer to whoever is talking, and the refusal to be handled. In a genre whose women were usually singing a chorus on somebody else’s record, she was the one making the statement and paying for the video.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Soy Mamá","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Soy Mamá, in 2022, produced by Leo RD, was remixed with Cardi B, Farina and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"439bbc6c-f06e-447f-a1fa-b2130b885457","displayText":"Yailin La Más Viral","occurrenceId":"0868e33f-8c3a-4070-bbb2-1a5b7ab02e2a"}},{"text":" — three women from three countries on a Dominican dembow record, which had not happened before at that level.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"It was nominated for best dembow collaboration at the Premios Juventud of 2023, in a category it did not win.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The Billboard stage","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In October 2025 she performed at the Latin Billboard awards in Miami for the first time, in a segment built as a tribute to Dominican music, singing Borrón y Cuenta Nueva beside Elvis Crespo and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"6fc762d4-96b8-4ecf-aca8-fdf52936658e","displayText":"Toño Rosario","occurrenceId":"db055583-0705-4cad-845f-cd83d7dbbe46"}},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Putting a dembowsera from Cristo Rey on that stage next to two merengue institutions is a piece of framing that would have been unthinkable a decade earlier, and it is a reasonable measure of what she has changed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The recent work — La Hoja Se Volteó, Dominguito, Ilusionada, Me Liberé, Ou Ou, Bandida, Presión, and Yo Lo Quiero with N-Fasis — keeps arriving, and the touring runs across the Dominican Republic, the United States and Europe.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '3b3385fb-7b5b-41c4-b027-7fd1741a1bb8';

-- la-insuperable [es]: 1
--   "Yailin la Más Viral" -> "Yailin La Más Viral"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"La Insuperable, de nombre Indhira Ircania Luna Guzmán, es cantante y compositora dominicana. Se crió en Cristo Rey, lleva quince años al frente del género, y durante buena parte de ese tiempo fue la única mujer que estaba ahí.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cero Gogas","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"No salió a grabar por su cuenta. En un evento a finales de 2007 alguien la oyó, le dijo que su imagen y su porte eran demasiado fuertes para desperdiciarlos, y la empujó a un estudio. Su propia objeción era que no le gustaba su voz.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cero Gogas salió en 2011, un mambo escrito por ella, y se movió el mismo día que apareció. Desde entonces escribe su material.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Que Me Den Banda","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Los sencillos van en línea sin cortes: Contigo Quiero Estar, Ojos Que No Ven Corazón Que No Siente, Nota Jamaiquina y Siempre Regia Nunca Camu en 2014, Escondió, No Me Niegue, La Que Ta Buena Soy Yo, después Que Me Den Banda y Cuál Es La Vaina en 2016, Me Subo Arriba en 2017, Cama Vacía en 2018, y Salao, Yo Soy Esa, Su Favorita y Tengo Derecho en 2019.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El asunto es casi siempre el mismo y vale nombrarlo: dinero propio, respuesta a quien esté hablando, y la negativa a dejarse manejar. En un género cuyas mujeres solían estar cantando el coro del disco de otro, ella era la que hacía la declaración y pagaba el video.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Soy Mamá","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Soy Mamá, de 2022, producida por Leo RD, se remezcló con Cardi B, Farina y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"439bbc6c-f06e-447f-a1fa-b2130b885457","displayText":"Yailin La Más Viral","occurrenceId":"aa85d6a2-8ae7-4e7e-b792-bed2718769ac"}},{"text":": tres mujeres de tres países sobre un disco de dembow dominicano, cosa que a ese nivel no había pasado.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Quedó nominada a mejor colaboración de dembow en los Premios Juventud de 2023, en un renglón que no ganó.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"La tarima de Billboard","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"En octubre de 2025 se presentó por primera vez en los premios Latin Billboard de Miami, en un segmento armado como homenaje a la música dominicana, cantando Borrón y Cuenta Nueva al lado de Elvis Crespo y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"6fc762d4-96b8-4ecf-aca8-fdf52936658e","displayText":"Toño Rosario","occurrenceId":"3e92e66f-2a2c-4593-a7ac-55e4e67963f0"}},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Poner a una dembowsera de Cristo Rey en esa tarima junto a dos instituciones del merengue es un encuadre que una década antes habría sido impensable, y es una medida razonable de lo que ella ha cambiado.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El trabajo reciente —La Hoja Se Volteó, Dominguito, Ilusionada, Me Liberé, Ou Ou, Bandida, Presión, y Yo Lo Quiero con N-Fasis— sigue llegando, y las giras cruzan República Dominicana, Estados Unidos y Europa.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '46e2b805-fce1-41d5-a135-2471cdefaab7';

-- puyalo-pantera [en]: 2
--   "Yailin la Más Viral" -> "Yailin La Más Viral"
--   "Yailin la Más Viral" -> "Yailin La Más Viral"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Puyalo Pantera is a Dominican dembow producer, songwriter and performer, born Jarlin José Beltre Lara. He produced Bing Bong, Chapa and Silla for ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"439bbc6c-f06e-447f-a1fa-b2130b885457","displayText":"Yailin La Más Viral","occurrenceId":"bc451ce0-bd2d-4517-8a16-d94d1e8f7589"}},{"text":", and Bing Bong is the record that carried Dominican dembow furthest in its year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"What he builds","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Apple Music describes his productions as rhythm in capital letters, and lists what goes into them: dembow, afro, trap, reggaetón, bachata. That is the working method of a modern Dominican producer — the dembow pulse is the floor, and everything else is a colour laid over it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Bing Bong is the clearest example. It is built on a two-syllable hook that carries no meaning at all, which is the point: dembow works by making a sound stick before a listener has decided whether to like it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Bing Bong","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"It charted internationally and it was nominated for Best Dembow Song at the Premio Lo Nuestro of 2026, in a category that also held Celos by ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3e1718be-c12d-42f5-85e7-2156d9574940","displayText":"Tokischa","occurrenceId":"96213174-15bd-4a1d-ad80-ebdcfdc45c86"}},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"550df3b5-6488-4aec-a476-a5d28d52ceea","displayText":"Bulin 47","occurrenceId":"5a87f6b8-0ad3-490b-9733-04a9f973decb"}},{"text":", and Dem Bow by Natti Natasha with Nando Boom.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The nomination named him alongside ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"439bbc6c-f06e-447f-a1fa-b2130b885457","displayText":"Yailin La Más Viral","occurrenceId":"38139394-59d1-48c8-95ce-45c69a753701"}},{"text":", which is unusual. Producers are ordinarily invisible at that level of the industry, and having the credit on the ballot is a measure of how much of the record was his.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The credit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In March 2025 he went public claiming credits and royalties on those records. The dispute has been reported in the Dominican press and argued in interviews, and it is not settled here.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"It is worth recording because it is the recurring problem of the genre. Dembow is made fast, often between people who are friends before they are business partners, and the paperwork tends to arrive after the record has already been heard by millions. Who wrote the hook is a question that only becomes expensive once the hook works.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The rest of the work","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He has recorded with La Perversa, released a dembow with Anyonca and Lomiel in July 2025, and has credits reaching across the field — ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"741eb4c0-4ab8-4ad5-8a64-2f156da6a395","displayText":"Ceky Viciny","occurrenceId":"36906c60-ea18-4417-b432-5f68ec86346a"}},{"text":"’s La Casa de Alofoke, La Gigi’s La Azarosa. He also releases under his own name.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'b8312af1-c310-4483-8d55-96415746d5f1';

-- puyalo-pantera [es]: 2
--   "Yailin la Más Viral" -> "Yailin La Más Viral"
--   "Yailin la Más Viral" -> "Yailin La Más Viral"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Puyalo Pantera es un productor, compositor e intérprete de dembow dominicano, nacido Jarlin José Beltre Lara. Produjo Bing Bong, Chapa y Silla para ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"439bbc6c-f06e-447f-a1fa-b2130b885457","displayText":"Yailin La Más Viral","occurrenceId":"3a853201-2e79-4958-a6f9-113f0ba24b32"}},{"text":", y Bing Bong es el disco que llevó el dembow dominicano más lejos en su año.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Lo que arma","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Apple Music describe sus producciones como ritmo con mayúsculas, y enumera lo que llevan dentro: dembow, afro, trap, reguetón, bachata. Ese es el método de trabajo de un productor dominicano de hoy: el pulso del dembow es el piso, y todo lo demás es color puesto encima.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Bing Bong es el ejemplo más claro. Está montada sobre un gancho de dos sílabas que no significa absolutamente nada, y de eso se trata: el dembow funciona haciendo que un sonido se pegue antes de que el oyente haya decidido si le gusta.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Bing Bong","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Sonó fuera del país y fue nominada a Mejor Canción Dembow en el Premio Lo Nuestro de 2026, en una categoría donde también estaban Celos, de ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3e1718be-c12d-42f5-85e7-2156d9574940","displayText":"Tokischa","occurrenceId":"7f1a5ecf-dd3d-4fc7-8f07-8049d5960004"}},{"text":" con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"550df3b5-6488-4aec-a476-a5d28d52ceea","displayText":"Bulin 47","occurrenceId":"5ed2d66b-dcf9-4528-a984-fde41a846b5d"}},{"text":", y Dem Bow, de Natti Natasha con Nando Boom.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"La nominación lo nombró junto a ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"439bbc6c-f06e-447f-a1fa-b2130b885457","displayText":"Yailin La Más Viral","occurrenceId":"9750f3c7-3406-4408-a3f1-000c452393e8"}},{"text":", y eso es raro. A ese nivel de la industria los productores son invisibles por costumbre, y que el crédito llegue a la papeleta mide cuánto del disco era suyo.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El crédito","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"En marzo de 2025 salió públicamente a reclamar créditos y regalías por esos discos. La prensa dominicana lo ha reportado y se ha discutido en entrevistas, y aquí no se resuelve.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Vale la pena anotarlo porque es el problema recurrente del género. El dembow se hace rápido, muchas veces entre gente que es amiga antes que socia, y el papeleo suele llegar después de que el disco ya lo oyeron millones. Quién escribió el gancho es una pregunta que solo se vuelve cara cuando el gancho funciona.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El resto del trabajo","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Ha grabado con La Perversa, sacó un dembow con Anyonca y Lomiel en julio de 2025, y tiene créditos por todo el campo: La Casa de Alofoke, de ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"741eb4c0-4ab8-4ad5-8a64-2f156da6a395","displayText":"Ceky Viciny","occurrenceId":"d04f349b-364d-42a5-946f-2e215921e9c7"}},{"text":", y La Azarosa, de La Gigi. También publica con su propio nombre.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '47ca8c26-b3ea-498e-8d5d-a755bfc3dd97';

-- r1-la-esencia [en]: 1
--   "Melymel" -> "MelyMel"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"R1 la Esencia is a Dominican rapper. He has been releasing records since the late two-thousands, which puts him among the people who were already working when Dominican urban music was still arguing about whether it existed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El Cuero","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The song that travelled is El Cuero, made with the Puerto Ricans Jowell & Randy, and a mambo remix of it followed in 2011. Getting a Puerto Rican duo of that size onto a Dominican rap record at that moment was not a routine booking — the traffic mostly ran the other way.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Yo Soy El R and Yo He Hecho De To are from the same period, and he put out a short documentary about himself around then too, which tells you he was already thinking of the career as something with a shape.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Who he records with","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The credits run across the whole Dominican field and across two generations of it. ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"102e7b78-ff98-4adc-9a54-ae73791fb176","displayText":"Lápiz Conciente","occurrenceId":"dcbf56e4-8f64-4eec-942c-0232e4d94dc0"}},{"text":" is on Calma. Di Lo Que Quieras puts him with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"47d07f5d-de56-4ad2-abc3-3e33221805dd","displayText":"Tali Goya","occurrenceId":"5d5f4063-75e8-4ae9-b667-82cdbf7d714b"}},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"de562eb7-a0fb-49c2-a3c8-db97a4be80e3","displayText":"Químico Ultra Mega","occurrenceId":"df931072-6ccb-4cf9-8019-ae1b5fe9e8c1"}},{"text":". Another track sets him beside Bulova, ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"580293c3-1e1e-418f-8555-8082665c22b0","displayText":"MelyMel","occurrenceId":"e8d1857d-5004-4216-b21c-9dc2cbe1133c"}},{"text":", Químico and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"9bf41d47-c5ab-45e3-b048-7bb7886b0912","displayText":"DJ Scuff","occurrenceId":"f00050cd-a9e4-4b17-a2f0-33781c927ae0"}},{"text":" at once.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Mueve El Culo, in 2018, went outside again — Jamby El Favo, Ñejo and Ecko, all Puerto Rican. KM and Bendiciones are more recent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Still on the bill","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He plays the diaspora circuit. A September date at the United Palace in New York had him billed with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"102e7b78-ff98-4adc-9a54-ae73791fb176","displayText":"Lápiz Conciente","occurrenceId":"7f2b6c7f-2f08-45c4-94b3-04c531c04968"}},{"text":" — two rappers from the same generation filling a theatre in Washington Heights, which is where a Dominican rap career of that vintage ends up if it lasts.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Almost nothing personal about him is on the record. No date of birth, no legal name, no town. He is documented entirely by who he has recorded with.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'd5cd4079-324b-40a3-a5a9-b93f4d6ea709';

-- r1-la-esencia [es]: 1
--   "Melymel" -> "MelyMel"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"R1 la Esencia es un rapero dominicano. Publica discos desde finales de los dos mil, lo que lo pone entre la gente que ya estaba trabajando cuando la música urbana dominicana todavía discutía si existía.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El Cuero","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"La canción que viajó es El Cuero, hecha con los puertorriqueños Jowell & Randy, y en 2011 salió un remix de mambo de ella. Meter a un dúo puertorriqueño de ese tamaño en un disco de rap dominicano en aquel momento no era una contratación rutinaria: el tráfico iba sobre todo al revés.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Yo Soy El R y Yo He Hecho De To son del mismo período, y por ahí también sacó un documental corto sobre sí mismo, lo que dice que ya pensaba la carrera como algo con forma.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Con quién graba","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Los créditos cruzan todo el campo dominicano y dos generaciones de él. ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"102e7b78-ff98-4adc-9a54-ae73791fb176","displayText":"Lápiz Conciente","occurrenceId":"480fe81f-b8f3-4072-8210-24b8891c7354"}},{"text":" está en Calma. Di Lo Que Quieras lo pone con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"47d07f5d-de56-4ad2-abc3-3e33221805dd","displayText":"Tali Goya","occurrenceId":"d7924585-431c-47bd-b1c3-8ad5655cf297"}},{"text":" y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"de562eb7-a0fb-49c2-a3c8-db97a4be80e3","displayText":"Químico Ultra Mega","occurrenceId":"ffe597c9-1cbe-4b12-84eb-ba04067b12c1"}},{"text":". Otro tema lo sienta a la vez al lado de Bulova, ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"580293c3-1e1e-418f-8555-8082665c22b0","displayText":"MelyMel","occurrenceId":"8a97dd4f-eb87-48d0-8c37-5f74c53bb922"}},{"text":", Químico y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"9bf41d47-c5ab-45e3-b048-7bb7886b0912","displayText":"DJ Scuff","occurrenceId":"23db1f65-f537-472e-b768-eb734b26b6fd"}},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Mueve El Culo, en 2018, volvió a salir afuera: Jamby El Favo, Ñejo y Ecko, todos puertorriqueños. KM y Bendiciones son más recientes.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Todavía en cartel","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Trabaja el circuito de la diáspora. Una fecha de septiembre en el United Palace de Nueva York lo puso en cartel con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"102e7b78-ff98-4adc-9a54-ae73791fb176","displayText":"Lápiz Conciente","occurrenceId":"3bed9db4-dbfd-4322-be7e-c1bc1d7c5530"}},{"text":": dos raperos de la misma generación llenando un teatro en Washington Heights, que es donde termina una carrera de rap dominicano de esa cosecha si dura.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"De lo personal casi no hay nada anotado. Ni fecha de nacimiento, ni nombre legal, ni pueblo. Está documentado enteramente por con quién ha grabado.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '40f1d816-c3c8-4492-978f-18e815b9bddf';

-- rocko-fara-on [en]: 1
--   "Fortuna La Super F" -> "Fortuna la Súper F"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Rocko & Fara-On are a Dominican urban duo. Their debut album, Los Verdaderos Tígueres, came out in November 2014 on EVT Music and Sony Music Latin after ten years of working underground.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Ten years to the first record","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El Caribe put it plainly at the end of that year: a decade of underground work and several proposals in the shadows came before anybody was listening. That is the ordinary shape of a Dominican urban career and it is rarely written down, because the story only becomes worth telling once the label signs.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The album went out exclusively on Spotify for its first week — a distribution decision that in 2014 was still novel enough to be reported as news — and carried guests from across the field: ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"1cd11a22-573a-43b4-8f54-fbd08329a4e2","displayText":"Ilegales","occurrenceId":"69fbf30c-c379-4091-af08-153be8f75a5f"}},{"text":", Vakero, Fulanito and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"858f68cd-dc14-497a-8391-c4a1b1777d27","displayText":"Fortuna la Súper F","occurrenceId":"d45ca2d8-f059-407d-8649-e86491ba8199"}},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Alza la Mano","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The single was built on the choruses of El General, the Panamanian who took Spanish-language dancehall to the whole continent in the early nineties. Clearing it was the hard part: Rocko has said permission had to come from Erick Morillo, from Robi Rob of C&C Music Factory and from Edgardo Franco himself.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The effort went well beyond what a hook normally requires. They were not sampling for texture; they were claiming a lineage, and they wanted the people who made it to sign off.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Between the merengue and the machine","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Dale Pa’ Tras is the one that travelled. It reached listeners of merengue and of electronic music at the same time, which is a hard trick and the reason Sony noticed them.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"They have described the problem themselves: audiences expect a tropical merengue from them, or a romantic urban reggaetón, and the records sit between those expectations rather than inside either. Chuiqui Chuiqui, Hay Que Vivir la Vida, No Me Aguanto, Ella Es Mía and a Christmas song, Pa’ Gozar Navidad, are the rest of it.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '5d36381d-db45-4dbb-9870-cd5f8be1cff0';

-- rocko-fara-on [es]: 1
--   "Fortuna La Super F" -> "Fortuna la Súper F"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Rocko & Fara-On son un dúo urbano dominicano. Su álbum de estreno, Los Verdaderos Tígueres, salió en noviembre de 2014 por EVT Music y Sony Music Latin, después de diez años de trabajo underground.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Diez años hasta el primer disco","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El Caribe lo dijo sin rodeos al cierre de aquel año: una década de trabajo underground y varias propuestas bajo la sombra vinieron antes de que alguien los estuviera oyendo. Esa es la forma corriente de una carrera urbana dominicana y casi nunca queda anotada, porque la historia solo se vuelve contable cuando firma el sello.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El álbum salió en exclusiva por Spotify durante su primera semana —una decisión de distribución que en 2014 todavía era bastante nueva como para ser noticia— y llevaba invitados de todo el campo: ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"1cd11a22-573a-43b4-8f54-fbd08329a4e2","displayText":"Ilegales","occurrenceId":"88e27bfd-92ce-4015-8f30-5a4f9de1a066"}},{"text":", Vakero, Fulanito y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"858f68cd-dc14-497a-8391-c4a1b1777d27","displayText":"Fortuna la Súper F","occurrenceId":"b4609133-d867-4d33-86df-956e98c1cb31"}},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Alza la Mano","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El sencillo está armado sobre los coros de El General, el panameño que llevó el dancehall en español a todo el continente a comienzos de los noventa. Lo difícil fue el permiso: Rocko ha contado que hubo que conseguirlo de Erick Morillo, de Robi Rob de C&C Music Factory y del propio Edgardo Franco.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El esfuerzo fue muy superior al que un gancho suele exigir. No estaban sampleando por textura; estaban reclamando una descendencia, y querían que la gente que la hizo diera el visto bueno.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Entre el merengue y la máquina","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Dale Pa’ Tras es el que viajó. Llegó a la vez a oyentes de merengue y a oyentes de música electrónica, que es un truco difícil y la razón por la que Sony se fijó en ellos.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Ellos mismos han descrito el problema: el público espera de ellos un merengue tropical o un reguetón romántico urbano, y los discos se quedan entre esas expectativas en vez de meterse en alguna. Chuiqui Chuiqui, Hay Que Vivir la Vida, No Me Aguanto, Ella Es Mía y un tema de Navidad, Pa’ Gozar Navidad, son el resto.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'e523d695-62cc-4cf8-b4b1-49f753de56f9';

-- roxy-espinal [en]: 1
--   "Rubby Perez" -> "Rubby Pérez"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Roxy Espinal is a Dominican singer and songwriter from Jarabacoa. She works in bolero, ranchera and ballad rather than in any of the genres her country is known for, and she has had to say so out loud more than once.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Jarabacoa and Mexico","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"She introduces herself the same way every time: a Dominican singer, straight from Jarabacoa, the city of eternal spring, with strong Mexican influences. The second half is the unusual part. Dominican audiences expect merengue, bachata or urbano from a young singer, and she has said plainly that some people do not realise she is Dominican because of what she chooses to sing.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Her answer has been that Mexican music has always had a place in her, the way it does for a great many Dominicans who grew up with it on the radio without anyone calling it foreign.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The songs","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Yo Primero He De Olvidarte, Soy Todo Lo Que Tengo, El Peso De Tu Ausencia and Aprender A Superar are her own. She has described one of them as written for the loves that never happened and hurt anyway, which is a fair statement of what the whole catalogue is about.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"She releases singles rather than albums, one at a time, each with a video.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The covers","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The other half of the work is other people’s songs, rebuilt. She made a Spanish version of Amy Winehouse’s Back To Black — her own translation, not an existing one — and it travelled further than anything else she has posted. She has also sung Camilo Sesto’s Madre and a tribute to Kany García.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"When ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"cff70c92-8632-4c66-b5a0-81622c8128b0","displayText":"Rubby Pérez","occurrenceId":"c67f7947-1348-4c64-b3a8-a928177a2203"}},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"6c3e0d74-23b7-4d80-969f-9d5319ee5127","displayText":"Alex Bueno","occurrenceId":"08349743-b5bf-41fc-96a5-bb32cef4ae46"}},{"text":" died she recorded homages to both. The Alex Bueno tribute reached a wide audience quickly, and the merengue repertoire suited her voice more easily than the distance between the two genres would suggest.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Live work","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"She plays restaurant and plaza dates at home and has taken the act to Punta Cana and to Medellín. It is a working singer’s calendar, built one room at a time.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'fae308f2-e009-4abd-9b59-73f638dacf50';

-- roxy-espinal [es]: 1
--   "Rubby Perez" -> "Rubby Pérez"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Roxy Espinal es una cantautora dominicana de Jarabacoa. Trabaja el bolero, la ranchera y la balada antes que cualquiera de los géneros por los que se conoce a su país, y ha tenido que decirlo en voz alta más de una vez.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Jarabacoa y México","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Se presenta igual cada vez: una cantante dominicana, directamente desde Jarabacoa, la ciudad de la eterna primavera, con fuertes influencias mexicanas. La segunda mitad es la parte rara. El público dominicano espera merengue, bachata o urbano de una cantante joven, y ella ha dicho sin rodeos que hay gente que no sabe que es dominicana por lo que escoge cantar.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su respuesta ha sido que la música mexicana siempre ha tenido un lugar en ella, como lo tiene para muchísimos dominicanos que crecieron con ella en la radio sin que nadie la llamara ajena.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Las canciones","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Yo Primero He De Olvidarte, Soy Todo Lo Que Tengo, El Peso De Tu Ausencia y Aprender A Superar son suyas. De una de ellas ha dicho que la escribió para los amores que nunca se dieron y que duelen igual, que es una descripción justa de lo que trata el catálogo entero.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Publica sencillos y no álbumes, de a uno, cada uno con su video.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Las versiones","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"La otra mitad del trabajo son canciones ajenas, rearmadas. Hizo una versión en español de Back To Black, de Amy Winehouse —traducción suya, no una ya existente— y llegó más lejos que ninguna otra cosa que haya subido. También ha cantado Madre, de Camilo Sesto, y un tributo a Kany García.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cuando murieron ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"cff70c92-8632-4c66-b5a0-81622c8128b0","displayText":"Rubby Pérez","occurrenceId":"7d015619-be26-460c-82a0-3bebf621b90e"}},{"text":" y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"6c3e0d74-23b7-4d80-969f-9d5319ee5127","displayText":"Alex Bueno","occurrenceId":"089916cd-c306-42cd-b8d5-673b0010a08a"}},{"text":" les grabó homenajes a los dos. El de Alex Bueno llegó rápido y lejos, y el repertorio merenguero le acomodó la voz con más facilidad de la que haría suponer la distancia entre los dos géneros.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El trabajo en vivo","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Toca fechas de restaurante y de plaza en el país, y ha llevado el espectáculo a Punta Cana y a Medellín. Es la agenda de una cantante de trabajo, armada sala por sala.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '846f1a44-3ee2-4ec8-ad18-31e5e185bd5e';

-- shadow-blow [en]: 1
--   "Yailin la Más Viral" -> "Yailin La Más Viral"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Shadow Blow, born José Ariel Fernández Soto, is a Dominican dembow singer, songwriter and producer. He spent ten years at the Conservatorio Nacional de Música and left it a classical pianist, which is not a sentence that fits anybody else in the genre.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The conservatory","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He was singing classical music from 1990. He plays piano, flute and percussion. When he decided to move into urban music his parents objected, and then supported him anyway, which is the ordinary shape of that conversation in a Dominican household where somebody has already paid for a decade of lessons.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His first group, in 2001, was Bloke C 37. Tú Eres La Que Está gave him a name in 2010; En El Área went nowhere and he stopped for a while.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Oh Oh","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He came back in 2014 with Oh Oh and brought fifteen symphonic musicians with him. Nobody in Dominican urban music had done that. What makes it more than a stunt is that he could write the parts himself.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Mensaje Directo became the record people quote back at him — a romantic lyric about an ordinary thing, which in a genre built on volume and provocation is the harder position to hold. Tú Con Él y Yo Con Ella, made with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"fa9cc802-28ca-4695-b585-f75aa90a2b6c","displayText":"Mozart La Para","occurrenceId":"3245b43d-e3c5-41d0-8e4a-afb249d7da7f"}},{"text":", took over Dominican radio.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Who he records with","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The credits go everywhere. Cambia de Planes with Bryant Myers and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"95e181f1-58e5-4537-a5e8-75a9f60c6aca","displayText":"Tivi Gunz","occurrenceId":"557a3e84-7396-4f0a-8bf5-edc144023cd6"}},{"text":"; La Que Me Mata and later ZAZA with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"6321da6c-e2d5-490a-a4e8-416bbee81edf","displayText":"Don Miguelo","occurrenceId":"33a986ae-f77d-4593-82d4-33a756067a1a"}},{"text":"; work through 2021 with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"741eb4c0-4ab8-4ad5-8a64-2f156da6a395","displayText":"Ceky Viciny","occurrenceId":"2f25535d-f5e6-40d3-b445-1a96ed948ff3"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"439bbc6c-f06e-447f-a1fa-b2130b885457","displayText":"Yailin La Más Viral","occurrenceId":"2383152d-e205-4fd8-8c6c-dfc0f577cd47"}},{"text":" and Brray; the En Filita Cooperando remix with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"97610f30-fb92-4d77-8b98-ddec14d12afc","displayText":"Musicólogo the Libro","occurrenceId":"b99eb484-ecce-446f-ae58-555dffa34931"}},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"d08f2c85-7f47-449e-a486-a19ba3b5841a","displayText":"La Insuperable","occurrenceId":"4fc38589-6008-44cd-a727-7fc6cf2666cf"}},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Saga, in 2023, is an album made jointly with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"102e7b78-ff98-4adc-9a54-ae73791fb176","displayText":"Lápiz Conciente","occurrenceId":"e7e5707c-9e42-4ce0-a0f7-0086d11ee8cc"}},{"text":" — one of the few times a rapper of that standing has shared a full record rather than a feature.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Eighteen songs in thirty-eight days","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He went into the second season of La Casa de Alofoke to prove he could produce, and came out having written and produced eighteen songs inside the house. One of them, La Piraña, started as an in-house joke with another contestant and was a real record before the season ended.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 2025 he performed at the Premio Nacional de la Juventud with the Orquesta Filarmónica del Caribe, in front of the country’s officials, doing on a national stage what he had first tried on Oh Oh eleven years earlier.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Flore came the same year. Regalo is from it.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'f48fd575-6cde-4c19-a214-d2fa92fd3897';

-- shadow-blow [es]: 1
--   "Yailin la Más Viral" -> "Yailin La Más Viral"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Shadow Blow, de nombre José Ariel Fernández Soto, es cantante, compositor y productor de dembow dominicano. Pasó diez años en el Conservatorio Nacional de Música y salió de ahí pianista clásico, que no es una frase que le quede a nadie más en el género.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El conservatorio","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cantaba música clásica desde 1990. Toca piano, flauta y percusión. Cuando decidió pasarse a la música urbana sus padres se molestaron, y después lo apoyaron igual, que es la forma corriente de esa conversación en una casa dominicana donde ya se pagó una década de clases.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su primer grupo, en 2001, fue Bloke C 37. Tú Eres La Que Está le dio nombre en 2010; En El Área no llegó a ningún lado y se detuvo un tiempo.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Oh Oh","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Volvió en 2014 con Oh Oh y se trajo quince músicos sinfónicos. Nadie en la música urbana dominicana había hecho eso. Lo que lo separa de una ocurrencia es que él podía escribir las partes.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Mensaje Directo se convirtió en el disco que la gente le devuelve: una letra romántica sobre algo corriente, que en un género montado sobre el volumen y la provocación es la posición más difícil de sostener. Tú Con Él y Yo Con Ella, hecha con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"fa9cc802-28ca-4695-b585-f75aa90a2b6c","displayText":"Mozart La Para","occurrenceId":"50d7d043-2169-4c65-8353-7f7cf8404e51"}},{"text":", se apoderó de la radio dominicana.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Con quién graba","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Los créditos van a todas partes. Cambia de Planes con Bryant Myers y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"95e181f1-58e5-4537-a5e8-75a9f60c6aca","displayText":"Tivi Gunz","occurrenceId":"fff578f6-c5cd-4761-ab9f-e3c5964c9188"}},{"text":"; La Que Me Mata y después ZAZA con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"6321da6c-e2d5-490a-a4e8-416bbee81edf","displayText":"Don Miguelo","occurrenceId":"1afbe7e3-ec88-4857-b61e-d4e9684399b6"}},{"text":"; trabajo a lo largo de 2021 con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"741eb4c0-4ab8-4ad5-8a64-2f156da6a395","displayText":"Ceky Viciny","occurrenceId":"70404321-9f5e-4c94-92fa-4bbdca8a7e58"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"439bbc6c-f06e-447f-a1fa-b2130b885457","displayText":"Yailin La Más Viral","occurrenceId":"c8e61ba4-b60d-4c53-a6c6-4003782a1e50"}},{"text":" y Brray; el remix de En Filita Cooperando con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"97610f30-fb92-4d77-8b98-ddec14d12afc","displayText":"Musicólogo the Libro","occurrenceId":"ecb0d290-41b8-454f-badf-a06a73729539"}},{"text":" y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"d08f2c85-7f47-449e-a486-a19ba3b5841a","displayText":"La Insuperable","occurrenceId":"6d972165-63aa-4a4f-8bc6-cae1617f6b31"}},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Saga, de 2023, es un álbum hecho a medias con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"102e7b78-ff98-4adc-9a54-ae73791fb176","displayText":"Lápiz Conciente","occurrenceId":"1e84a338-1c01-4292-8acb-25472ba33087"}},{"text":": una de las pocas veces que un rapero de ese peso comparte un disco entero en vez de una participación.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Dieciocho canciones en treinta y ocho días","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Entró a la segunda temporada de La Casa de Alofoke a demostrar que podía producir, y salió habiendo escrito y producido dieciocho canciones dentro de la casa. Una de ellas, La Piraña, empezó como un chiste interno con otro concursante y ya era un disco de verdad antes de que terminara la temporada.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"En 2025 se presentó en el Premio Nacional de la Juventud con la Orquesta Filarmónica del Caribe, delante de las autoridades del país, haciendo en una tarima nacional lo que había ensayado once años antes en Oh Oh.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Flore salió ese mismo año. Regalo es de ahí.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'fcc83780-cb45-4e85-85aa-57f4cce44e2f';

-- sony-ovalles [en]: 1
--   "Milly, Jocelyn y los Vecinos" -> "Milly, Jocelyn y Los Vecinos"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Sony Ovalles","type":"text"},{"text":" was one of the most prolific, versatile, and influential pianists, arrangers, composers, and musical directors in the history of Dominican popular music. Known as an essential figure during the golden age of merengue and tropical music throughout the 1970s, 1980s, and 1990s, his work behind the scenes defined the signature sound of dozens of Latin music classics.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Early Life and Academic Background","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Born in the Dominican Republic into a family with deep musical roots, Ovalles displayed extraordinary talent for the piano at an early age. He pursued formal studies in music theory, sight-reading, counterpoint, and harmony, building a rigorous academic foundation. This preparation allowed him to seamlessly bridge classical music structures with complex jazz harmonies and Caribbean popular rhythms.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Career and Impact as a Producer and Arranger","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"During the height of the tropical music boom, Sony Ovalles became one of the most sought-after studio arrangers and session pianists in the Caribbean and Latin America. His work in recording studios helped shape the modern sonic identity of merengue, elevating its harmonic complexity while keeping its danceable core intact.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"As a master pianist and arranger, he collaborated with top-tier orchestras and prominent figures across tropical genres:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"2bc36959-dcce-4e10-9ecf-2cd418eaa489","displayText":"Wilfrido Vargas","occurrenceId":"9abac457-5770-47ee-a85a-da6b954d33e4"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Served as key pianist and arranger during crucial periods of the band''s international expansion.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"fb2c703f-5362-47dd-ada0-7c6d5e106f3b","displayText":"Dioni Fernández y El Equipo","occurrenceId":"37eed1ba-36da-473c-a250-68c5fd8252aa"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Crafted intricate brass and piano arrangements for the group''s classic catalog.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"703e412f-92ba-4ad8-a84c-54adc831c5e6","displayText":"El Zafiro","occurrenceId":"da002572-1247-46bf-a121-8833179ff735"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Produced and arranged major hits during the artist''s commercial peak in the 1980s.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"6c3e0d74-23b7-4d80-969f-9d5319ee5127","displayText":"Alex Bueno","occurrenceId":"3b751a85-b7aa-4e80-9f89-14b55a232535"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Developed musical arrangements for several of his hit merengue albums and romantic projects.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"241703d2-a7f0-457d-b020-704f3a02b0d4","displayText":"Milly, Jocelyn y Los Vecinos","occurrenceId":"e6dd7bb1-4333-47e3-9180-0a21f2d15aaf"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Worked closely with the group to develop polished, high-energy arrangements.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"059a9e99-5d11-433e-97b9-9c35e57908f1","displayText":"Sergio Vargas","occurrenceId":"e3f0821d-e45c-45c7-8763-6ff39e0ca39f"}},{"text":" & ","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"9da4edbe-7d7e-4bf1-8c23-ee01bb5ee65b","displayText":"El Jeffrey","occurrenceId":"b9d8e015-4a38-4340-ad15-e6df10b660c2"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Contributed arrangements and studio production for multiple tracks in their repertoires.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His versatility also led him to collaborate with international icons across salsa and Latin pop, contributing arrangements or performing on tracks alongside legendary artists such as Celia Cruz, Cheo Feliciano, and José José.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Musical Style and Innovations","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Sony Ovalles was renowned among musicians for his technical mastery and signature studio style:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Jazz-Infused Harmonies:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" He introduced sophisticated chord extensions, substitutions, and voicings into traditional tropical rhythm sections.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Virtuosic Piano Tumbaos:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" His piano lines were marked by extraordinary speed, rhythmic precision, and rich harmonic fills.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Dynamic Brass Orchestration:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" His writing for trumpets, saxophones, and trombones featured complex counterpoint and a bright, powerful ensemble tone.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Genre Adaptability:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" He moved effortlessly between high-tempo merengues, romantic ballads, boleros, salsa, and bachata.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Legacy","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Sony Ovalles is remembered as the \"silent architect\" behind many of the biggest hits in tropical music history. Preferring to focus his career on studio production, composition, and musical direction rather than front-stage celebrity, his vast catalog of arrangements remains an essential subject of study for Latin jazz and tropical music pianists worldwide.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '7e4e6c98-636e-4722-bb2f-7cc4a8ac7f45';

-- sony-ovalles [es]: 1
--   "Milly, Jocelyn y los Vecinos" -> "Milly, Jocelyn y Los Vecinos"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Sony Ovalles","type":"text"},{"text":" fue uno de los pianistas, arreglistas, compositores y directores de orquesta más prolíficos e influyentes en la historia de la música popular dominicana, considerado una pieza clave en la época de oro del merengue y la salsa en el Caribe.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Primeros años y formación","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Nació en la República Dominicana en el seno de una familia con fuerte tradición musical. Desde temprana edad mostró un talento excepcional para el piano, realizando estudios formales de teoría musical, solfeo y armonía. Su sólida preparación académica le permitió dominar tanto las estructuras de la música clásica como la libertad de la composición popular y el jazz.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Trayectoria profesional y el \"sonido del merengue\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"A lo largo de las décadas de 1970, 1980 y 1990, Sony Ovalles se convirtió en uno de los arreglistas más solicitados del área del Caribe. Su trabajo de estudio definió la sonoridad de decenas de producciones discográficas que alcanzaron éxito internacional.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Como arreglista y pianista, colaboró con las orquestas y artistas más importantes de la música tropical, entre los que destacan:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"2bc36959-dcce-4e10-9ecf-2cd418eaa489","displayText":"Wilfrido Vargas","occurrenceId":"75a0d200-204b-4151-9d7e-19016318ba1c"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Participó como pianista y arreglista en etapas fundamentales de la consolidación del estilo moderno del grupo.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"fb2c703f-5362-47dd-ada0-7c6d5e106f3b","displayText":"Dioni Fernández y El Equipo","occurrenceId":"1ff6c225-7754-406b-a74a-4eca0b10223c"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Elaboró arreglos emblemáticos para la agrupación.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"703e412f-92ba-4ad8-a84c-54adc831c5e6","displayText":"El Zafiro","occurrenceId":"f585e5bf-1c83-4c6b-a424-cb6ea9c1e323"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Responsable de los arreglos de grandes éxitos de la época.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"6c3e0d74-23b7-4d80-969f-9d5319ee5127","displayText":"Alex Bueno","occurrenceId":"4459367d-5aa6-4f2a-af27-eca258f58cf6"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Creó estructuras musicales para varias de sus producciones de merengue y música romántica.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"241703d2-a7f0-457d-b020-704f3a02b0d4","displayText":"Milly, Jocelyn y Los Vecinos","occurrenceId":"76933607-1416-4d83-9e72-9568bf382a05"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Colaboró en la elaboración del repertorio de la orquesta.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"059a9e99-5d11-433e-97b9-9c35e57908f1","displayText":"Sergio Vargas","occurrenceId":"f36161a6-0e4f-4d27-97f9-54ef3327b46a"}},{"text":" y ","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"9da4edbe-7d7e-4bf1-8c23-ee01bb5ee65b","displayText":"El Jeffrey","occurrenceId":"d1d951d8-6772-436a-8177-6dbbf6499a7b"}},{"text":":","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Produjo y arregló múltiples temas de sus catálogos.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su versatilidad le permitió trabajar con artistas internacionales de la salsa y la balada, dejando su huella en grabaciones de figuras como Celia Cruz, Cheo Feliciano y José José en sus incursiones o colaboraciones con ritmos caribeños.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Estilo e innovación musical","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El estilo de Sony Ovalles en el piano y en la orquestación se caracterizó por:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Un uso innovador de las armonías del jazz aplicadas a la base rítmica del merengue y la salsa.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Tumbaos de piano complejos, con gran velocidad, precisión armónica y sentido del ritmo.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Arreglos de metales (trompetas, trombones y saxofones) caracterizados por líneas contrapuntísticas y sonoridad brillante.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Adaptabilidad para arreglar desde merengues acelerados hasta baladas, boleros y bachatas de gran sofisticación.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Legado","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Sony Ovalles es recordado como el \"arquitecto silencioso\" detrás de muchos de los mayores éxitos del merengue clásico. Aunque prefirió mantener un perfil enfocado en el trabajo de estudio y la dirección musical detrás de escena más que en los focos de la fama, su catálogo de arreglos sigue siendo material de estudio obligatorio para pianistas y orquestadores de la música latina.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '8383d938-d4aa-4573-a1dd-814aeb4fc1c6';

-- the-new-york-band [en]: 1
--   " Wilfrido Vargas" -> "Wilfrido Vargas"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The New York Band is one of the most influential groups in the history of modern merengue, and the one that best represented, from the mid-1980s onward, the sound and attitude of the Dominican diaspora in New York. With a proposal that combined merengue with disco, funk and danceable pop, along with choreography and costumes closer to international pop than to traditional merengue, the orchestra opened a new way of understanding the genre for the young audiences of the era.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Origins and Founding","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The group was born in 1986 in New York City, driven by Dominican entrepreneur and artistic promoter ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"42f4687d-840e-4092-8899-eb8b8d9f5986","displayText":"Chery Jiménez y su Tercera Brigada","occurrenceId":"82143cad-2ebb-4e8f-8d46-469b5517be4a"}},{"text":", with the support of musician","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"2bc36959-dcce-4e10-9ecf-2cd418eaa489","displayText":"Wilfrido Vargas","occurrenceId":"fc80626b-7f90-405f-b7ec-87f154f211a4"}},{"text":", whose name appeared alongside the orchestra''s on the cover of their first album. It emerged as part of a wave of merengue groups formed by Dominicans based in that city, alongside projects like ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"02b306b3-acc0-4800-b314-05683205d1c5","displayText":"Patrulla 15","occurrenceId":"082c2d68-4acd-4fe8-a2f6-11d1440cdf1b"}},{"text":", at a moment when the Dominican community in New York was seeking a band that reflected its own cultural identity.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"First Lineup and Style","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In its early years, the group''s principal voice was singer Magda Lake. Shortly after, Irisneyda joined, originally from Santo Domingo, who had participated in a national talent competition before joining the orchestra. Musical direction and trombone were handled by Franklin Rivers, while Bolivita Quiñones provided the brass and woodwind sections and Anibalito Rijo handled keyboards and electric guitar. The group distinguished itself from the start through the quality of its instrumental execution and a choreographed stage presentation, with Alexandra as a notable dancer and backing vocalist.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"35b79560-87dc-47aa-9f52-dda21faec41f","displayText":"Cherito","occurrenceId":"30650ad8-e6d1-4773-bf41-d90193b2f699"}},{"text":"''s Arrival and Consolidation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 1988, ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"35b79560-87dc-47aa-9f52-dda21faec41f","displayText":"Cherito","occurrenceId":"7748a9da-6772-42da-ac84-f91557d055ca"}},{"text":" joined as a backing vocalist and soon became the lead voice. Along with Irisneyda, he became one of the main faces of the group, contributing his own compositions that quickly became part of the band''s repertoire. Under this lineup, The New York Band came to be considered the first merengue orchestra seriously aimed at the concert and theatrical spectacle format, not just the dance hall circuit, which set it apart from other groups of its time.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Hits and International Reach","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Their self-titled debut album, The New York Band (Karen Records, 1987), mixed merengue with disco, funk and pop rap, an unusual fusion for the genre at that moment. From there came songs like \"Colé,\" featuring Magda Lake on lead vocals. With ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"35b79560-87dc-47aa-9f52-dda21faec41f","displayText":"Cherito","occurrenceId":"c610888e-def7-48ba-b9db-1d55469844b2"}},{"text":" as frontman, the group solidified hits such as \"Si tú no estás,\" \"Nadie como tú\" — which gave its name to their second album, released in 1989 — \"Dame vida,\" \"Si tú eres mi hombre,\" \"Dancing Mood\" and \"Amada mía.\" The orchestra sold more than 2.5 million records and reached markets including Puerto Rico, the United States, Mexico, Colombia, Venezuela and Spain, winning recognition in several of those countries.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Lineup Changes and Dissolution","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"By the mid-1990s, internal tensions — including the decision to position ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"35b79560-87dc-47aa-9f52-dda21faec41f","displayText":"Cherito","occurrenceId":"0955507b-fd3a-4a08-af78-63154db27b2c"}},{"text":" as the group''s primary face above the other members — were distancing the original members. ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"35b79560-87dc-47aa-9f52-dda21faec41f","displayText":"Cherito","occurrenceId":"2a14b9b4-50fc-4a30-8e51-cd52f584fb60"}},{"text":" stepped away from the group in 1995 to launch his own solo career, and although The New York Band continued to perform for a time with other members, it eventually formally disbanded in 1998, closing its most remembered era.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The 2016 Reunion","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"After 23 years without performing together, much of the original lineup — Irisneyda, Franklin Rivers, Magda Lake, Johnny Contreras, ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"35b79560-87dc-47aa-9f52-dda21faec41f","displayText":"Cherito","occurrenceId":"89810d96-b67b-4fe8-845b-0005ad54fab0"}},{"text":", Alexandra, Miosotis and Tony — reunited on May 31, 2016, at the National Theater, where the orchestra received the Soberano Merit Award. The performance generated a euphoric response from the audience and shortly after resulted in their own tour, the \"Re:Encuentro World Tour,\" which once again brought the group to the most important stages in the country and across several Latin American cities.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"After Cherito: Continuity and Disputes","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"35b79560-87dc-47aa-9f52-dda21faec41f","displayText":"Cherito","occurrenceId":"233c787b-d267-462f-8323-7bc137ea82a0"}},{"text":"''s death in July 2019 left the orchestra without its most recognized face in recent decades and opened a dispute over the use of \"The New York Band\" name between","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"42f4687d-840e-4092-8899-eb8b8d9f5986","displayText":"Chery Jiménez y su Tercera Brigada","occurrenceId":"506e0926-89b9-41a0-bbd3-e4f1872978af"}},{"text":" and several members who wished to continue performing under that brand. Despite this, the group, led by Irisneyda and Franklin Rivers, maintained its activity and offered in March 2021 its first virtual concert, \"The New York Band para el mundo,\" with a lineup that included Johnny Contreras, Rubén Ariel, Tony Swing and newest member Maité, along with a special appearance by Alexandra as a guest.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Discography","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Among the most notable albums in their catalog are their self-titled debut, The New York Band (Karen Records, 1987), and Nadie Como Tú (1989), followed by other productions over more than a decade of recording activity.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Legacy","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The New York Band is remembered as one of the groups that redefined merengue in the 1980s and 90s, fusing it with international sounds and taking it from the dance hall to the concert stage. Figures like ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"35b79560-87dc-47aa-9f52-dda21faec41f","displayText":"Cherito","occurrenceId":"eda53891-cfaf-43cf-a309-ecfc48b53b59"}},{"text":" emerged from their ranks, and their catalog of hits remains part of the essential repertoire of Dominican merengue''s golden age.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'b062f19c-9465-413e-956b-a0f628a797ee';

-- tivi-gunz [en]: 1
--   "Yailin la Más Viral" -> "Yailin La Más Viral"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Tivi Gunz, born Stivi Randy Álvarez in Cristo Rey, is a Dominican rapper and songwriter. He moves between trap, rap and hip hop without settling in any of them, and he has been given a second name — Tinta de Oro — for how his verses are put together rather than for how they are delivered.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cristo Rey","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"I Love U was the first thing he put out. Tinta de Oro was the first album, and the phrase stuck to him afterwards.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He came up through the same capital street circuit that produces most Dominican rap, and he has told the story of establishing himself in la 42, in Capotillo, at length and more than once. That is not a metaphor for a career — in Dominican urban music a street with a scene attached is a market, and taking one is the whole first act.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Pícala","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 2018 he cut Pícala with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3e1718be-c12d-42f5-85e7-2156d9574940","displayText":"Tokischa","occurrenceId":"a5bb894e-23b5-4509-8ba4-ef6193a46b48"}},{"text":". It was her first release, and it went past a million views in its first week. Whatever else he has done, that record is a hinge in Dominican music, and he is on the other side of it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The credits since run across the field and out of it: ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"de562eb7-a0fb-49c2-a3c8-db97a4be80e3","displayText":"Químico Ultra Mega","occurrenceId":"0ac82b75-2568-4822-8a16-421b59182d2c"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"6321da6c-e2d5-490a-a4e8-416bbee81edf","displayText":"Don Miguelo","occurrenceId":"0421f1ed-749c-41ec-951b-594f60938ae7"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"439bbc6c-f06e-447f-a1fa-b2130b885457","displayText":"Yailin La Más Viral","occurrenceId":"6132e955-a14c-4061-8eb8-2dc8b73b1349"}},{"text":", Treintisiete and Onguito Wa at home; Ozuna on El Bloke; Casper Mágico and Nio García on the Selena remix; Justin Quiles, Tito El Bambino, Slayter and Trappa elsewhere.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The year of the weekly single","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 2023 he released a song every week for the whole year. Gass Pure, Zuculento, Kick Con El Bajo, Como Ella Baila Así, Lokisla, Pá La 42, Soga and Que La Gualle are the ones that stuck.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zuculento took urban collaboration of the year at the Glamour Music Awards. He performed at the Monitor Latino Awards in Santa Marta and at Premios Juventud, made his first tours of Europe and the United States, and headlined the thirty-fourth Gran Parada Dominicana del Bronx on the Grand Concourse.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"A weekly release schedule is a strategy, not a flourish: it treats the catalogue as something to be fed rather than curated, which is how a rapper stays on a platform’s algorithm without a label paying for him. That he could sustain it for fifty-two weeks says something about the writing.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Selena and after","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Selena, Chuky 2, Coronao, Therian, Rubberz, Mentiras and Culpable are more recent. The freestyles keep coming too, which is where the Tinta de Oro name was earned in the first place.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'acc464ef-2c82-43d0-8713-98db881a5deb';

-- tivi-gunz [es]: 1
--   "Yailin la Más Viral" -> "Yailin La Más Viral"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Tivi Gunz, de nombre Stivi Randy Álvarez y nacido en Cristo Rey, es rapero y compositor dominicano. Se mueve entre el trap, el rap y el hip hop sin quedarse en ninguno, y le pusieron un segundo nombre —Tinta de Oro— por cómo arma los versos y no por cómo los dice.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cristo Rey","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"I Love U fue lo primero que sacó. Tinta de Oro fue el primer álbum, y de ahí en adelante la frase se le quedó pegada.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Se formó en el mismo circuito de calle de la capital que produce casi todo el rap dominicano, y ha contado largo y más de una vez cómo se estableció en la 42, en Capotillo. Eso no es una metáfora de carrera: en la música urbana dominicana una calle con escena encima es un mercado, y tomarse una es el primer acto completo.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Pícala","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"En 2018 grabó Pícala con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3e1718be-c12d-42f5-85e7-2156d9574940","displayText":"Tokischa","occurrenceId":"30f65467-0148-4fd3-bc8f-e80f0788cd36"}},{"text":". Era el primer lanzamiento de ella, y pasó el millón de vistas en la primera semana. Haya hecho lo que haya hecho después, ese disco es una bisagra de la música dominicana, y él está del otro lado.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Los créditos desde entonces cruzan todo el campo y se salen de él: ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"de562eb7-a0fb-49c2-a3c8-db97a4be80e3","displayText":"Químico Ultra Mega","occurrenceId":"9e5db94b-2c7b-4d8f-9bb7-d74763841f4d"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"6321da6c-e2d5-490a-a4e8-416bbee81edf","displayText":"Don Miguelo","occurrenceId":"60a62104-8814-4bfb-8bed-639b9a02bdc1"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"439bbc6c-f06e-447f-a1fa-b2130b885457","displayText":"Yailin La Más Viral","occurrenceId":"26f6dca8-544b-4f14-b1f6-7217a10d003d"}},{"text":", Treintisiete y Onguito Wa aquí; Ozuna en El Bloke; Casper Mágico y Nio García en el remix de Selena; Justin Quiles, Tito El Bambino, Slayter y Trappa por fuera.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El año del sencillo semanal","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"En 2023 sacó una canción por semana durante todo el año. Gass Pure, Zuculento, Kick Con El Bajo, Como Ella Baila Así, Lokisla, Pá La 42, Soga y Que La Gualle son las que quedaron.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zuculento se llevó la colaboración urbana del año en los Glamour Music Awards. Se presentó en los Monitor Latino Awards de Santa Marta y en Premios Juventud, hizo sus primeras giras por Europa y Estados Unidos, y encabezó la trigésimo cuarta Gran Parada Dominicana del Bronx en el Grand Concourse.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Publicar semanalmente es una estrategia y no un adorno: trata el catálogo como algo que hay que alimentar y no que curar, que es como un rapero se mantiene en el algoritmo de una plataforma sin un sello pagándole. Que lo haya sostenido cincuenta y dos semanas dice algo sobre la escritura.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Selena y después","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Selena, Chuky 2, Coronao, Therian, Rubberz, Mentiras y Culpable son más recientes. Los freestyles también siguen saliendo, que es donde se ganó el nombre de Tinta de Oro en primer lugar.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '64246ff5-b636-4862-be08-d43000b73ad4';

-- twovm-para-cristo [en]: 1
--   "BS El Ideologo" -> "BS El Ideólogo"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"TwoVm Para Cristo is a Dominican Christian urban duo formed in Santo Domingo in 2009 by the brothers Josué Abel Guzmán Cruz and Ramón Emilio Guzmán Cruz. The name is a contraction of Dos Vencedores Más — two more victors — and they have released their records themselves rather than through a label.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Before the first album","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"They started out on local concert bills alongside other artists coming up at the same time, appearing on Qué Tu Va Hacer Papá with Raynelyz López and Eres Tú with Rivelking Jiménez before they had a record of their own.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Todo Tiene Su Tiempo","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The debut came in 2009 and set the pattern: a Christian lyric carried on tropical rhythms rather than on the straight reggaeton the duo is usually filed under. It took them out of the country that same year on their first South American tour.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Tour","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The second album, Tour, arrived in 2016 — ten songs and three videos, recorded in the Dominican Republic and led by the single Solo Llora, with Lyrical Colimbo and GL Layu appearing on it. In 2021 they were nominated for best urban duo at Premios El Galardón. A third project, Electronic, has been in progress since, trailed by the singles Vida, Para Gozar, Poderoso Gigante and La Reversa.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The other career","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Josué Guzmán also records on his own as ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"e0bde8f5-859f-435e-98ed-d59be5e74a78","displayText":"BS El Ideólogo","occurrenceId":"88936119-8685-4b35-a857-db93893d86bb"}},{"text":", working as a producer and rapper in secular dembow. The two catalogues run in parallel under different names and to different audiences. In Dominican music the Christian and secular scenes are usually treated as separate worlds; here they are the same man.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'deb257f4-ffb0-4265-875a-26cadd7e36b5';

-- twovm-para-cristo [es]: 1
--   "BS El Ideologo" -> "BS El Ideólogo"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"TwoVm Para Cristo es un dúo urbano cristiano dominicano formado en Santo Domingo en 2009 por los hermanos Josué Abel Guzmán Cruz y Ramón Emilio Guzmán Cruz. El nombre es una contracción de Dos Vencedores Más, y han publicado sus discos por cuenta propia y no a través de un sello.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Antes del primer disco","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Empezaron en carteles de conciertos locales junto a otros artistas que subían al mismo tiempo, apareciendo en Qué Tu Va Hacer Papá con Raynelyz López y en Eres Tú con Rivelking Jiménez antes de tener disco propio.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Todo Tiene Su Tiempo","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El debut llegó en 2009 y fijó la pauta: una letra cristiana montada sobre ritmos tropicales antes que sobre el reguetón puro en el que se les suele clasificar. Ese mismo año los sacó del país en su primera gira por Suramérica.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Tour","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El segundo álbum, Tour, salió en 2016: diez canciones y tres videos, grabados en la República Dominicana y encabezados por el sencillo Solo Llora, con Lyrical Colimbo y GL Layu como invitados. En 2021 fueron nominados a mejor dúo urbano en los Premios El Galardón. Un tercer proyecto, Electronic, sigue en curso, anticipado por los sencillos Vida, Para Gozar, Poderoso Gigante y La Reversa.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"La otra carrera","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Josué Guzmán graba además por su cuenta como ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"e0bde8f5-859f-435e-98ed-d59be5e74a78","displayText":"BS El Ideólogo","occurrenceId":"f14545ac-5ae5-478f-a688-6cc72eda4c92"}},{"text":", trabajando como productor y rapero en el dembow secular. Los dos catálogos corren en paralelo bajo nombres distintos y ante públicos distintos. En la música dominicana lo cristiano y lo secular suelen tratarse como mundos aparte, y aquí son el mismo hombre.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'e6017209-417b-4cf3-909a-0b79bc7db87d';

-- victor-waill [en]: 1
--   "Michel El Buenón" -> "Michel el Buenón"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Víctor Waill","type":"text"},{"text":", the stage name of Víctor Manuel Pérez Lugo, was one of the most versatile and essential musical creators of the Dominican Republic. Born on March 27, 1952, in Baní, he passed away on July 8, 2019, in Santo Domingo. He stood out with equal brilliance as a singer, arranger, producer, and multi-instrumentalist capable of mastering the bass, piano, and percussion. His revolutionary vision of Caribbean rhythms led him to become the mind behind massive merengue hits in the 1970s and 1980s, before eventually being recognized as a foundational figure and master of modern Dominican salsa.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Origins and early development","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"He was born in the municipality of Baní, in the province of Peravia. From a very young age, he manifested an innate intuition for music, teaching himself to play various string instruments, keyboards, and traditional percussion. He began his formal artistic career in 1974 by joining the local group Tino Peña y su combo salsa. It was during a performance with this ensemble that he caught the attention of the celebrated orchestra director ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"2bc36959-dcce-4e10-9ecf-2cd418eaa489","displayText":"Wilfrido Vargas","occurrenceId":"b55e205e-8f43-443a-bdb2-413e5f809ac0"}},{"text":", who immediately detected his immense potential as a front-line vocalist and musician, inviting him to join his legendary musical institution.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Musical career","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"His time with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"2bc36959-dcce-4e10-9ecf-2cd418eaa489","displayText":"Wilfrido Vargas","occurrenceId":"57d3d1a9-96bf-40d0-88c2-67a5ee4b5fb5"}},{"text":" consolidated his status, popularizing classic merengue and salsa tracks that defined an era, such as Charo, charo, Cachita, Enrique Blanco, and Sansón batalla, a piece that reached the top of the charts in Caribbean markets. Later on, he founded his own musical group called Panela, expanding his impact on the dance floors and gaining recognition in international venues.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"However, he experienced his most prolific phase from the recording studio as the main arranger and producer for an entire generation of artists. He was the sonic architect behind the hit catalog of the urban pop group ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"1cd11a22-573a-43b4-8f54-fbd08329a4e2","displayText":"Ilegales","occurrenceId":"413eb59d-cb31-406b-8bac-902218ca0561"}},{"text":", designed historic arrangements for figures of the stature of ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"bc310977-31a9-41bb-9af2-7d3a0d7fabdd","displayText":"Fernando Villalona","occurrenceId":"fd4cb7c5-61e2-42f7-8e47-cd7826e7af70"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3422883e-7048-48af-bb03-c68c8c557ee4","displayText":"Los Hermanos Rosario","occurrenceId":"7de5374c-44b8-4692-9560-8f14c8b56cc3"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"ae3c0afb-0e0a-4506-bbe6-a59c3c68bb1e","displayText":"Eddy Herrera","occurrenceId":"fc383a8a-a81b-43da-a3e8-f1078c5a17ee"}},{"text":", and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3f8bafec-e5ee-415d-8405-9551cceeeb9b","displayText":"Johnny Ventura","occurrenceId":"a21886d9-932a-4831-8689-b42c4d938fc9"}},{"text":", and spearheaded the massive resurgence of salsa produced in the Dominican Republic by mentoring, arranging, and producing the biggest hits for contemporary performers like ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"14777546-5b72-4dcf-b18a-15320124a0ee","displayText":"Alex Matos","occurrenceId":"5c674b29-abb0-4852-b85d-785775e80be0"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"969a3c82-6b31-4ea0-878d-8643001ca839","displayText":"Yanfourd","occurrenceId":"9b415c1a-9d0a-4fc3-bd93-703f53ee0dc0"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"69ca4e3b-2a3a-4e61-a5bd-2210606c5f13","displayText":"David Kada","occurrenceId":"e4b65146-0ce3-41f6-a36e-1354c3e18de8"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"d9ef5d29-573a-4812-b717-18a783d95a70","displayText":"Michel el Buenón","occurrenceId":"bb3096f0-b69c-48d4-ada9-7b5b29c394d4"}},{"text":", and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"33a84471-9f35-4a4f-9b56-b6a61e13212a","displayText":"Revolución Salsera","occurrenceId":"8fb2d256-c141-420f-844b-78affbe364d2"}},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Musical style and cultural impact","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"The style of Víctor Waill was characterized by his ability to fuse harmonic sophistication with the rhythmic power of the Caribbean. In the realm of Dominican salsa, his distinctive trademark consisted of introducing highly aggressive and modern brass sections combined with lyrics of a deeply romantic nature, giving a unique identity to the genre that competed with traditional powerhouses in the region. His versatility allowed him to jump from danceable merengue to ballads with absolute naturalness, earning the unanimous respect of his colleagues for his technical rigor at the mixing consoles and his sharp ear for identifying the commercial potential of a song.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Legacy","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"Víctor Waill is remembered as one of the most brilliant and generous minds in the Caribbean music industry, characterized by constantly driving the talent of new performers. His unexpected passing occurred at his residence in Santo Domingo due to sudden health complications, leaving the country in a deep state of shock. His physical departure left a void in the tropical scene, but his work remains intact through the hundreds of musical arrangements that continue to play daily on international radio stations and in the standard of quality he established for contemporary salsa.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '67312e71-4db9-482d-8206-b0b46840ffa2';

-- victor-waill [es]: 1
--   "Michel El Buenón" -> "Michel el Buenón"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Víctor Waill","type":"text"},{"text":", nombre artístico de Víctor Manuel Pérez Lugo, fue uno de los creadores musicales más polifacéticos de la República Dominicana. Nació el 27 de marzo de 1952 en Baní y falleció el 8 de julio de 2019 en Santo Domingo. Destacó como cantante, compositor, arreglista y multiinstrumentista capaz de dominar el bajo, el piano y la percusión. Su visión revolucionaria de los ritmos caribeños lo llevó a convertirse en la mente detrás de enormes éxitos del merengue en los años setenta y ochenta, para luego ser catalogado como una de las figuras clave y maestro de la salsa dominicana moderna.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Orígenes y desarrollo temprano","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"Nació en el municipio de Baní, dentro de la provincia de Peravia. Desde muy joven manifestó una intuición innata para la música, aprendiendo a ejecutar de manera autodidacta diversos instrumentos de cuerda, teclado y percusión tradicional. Inició su trayectoria artística formal en 1974 al integrarse a la agrupación local Tino Peña y su combo salsa. Fue durante una presentación con este conjunto que llamó la atención del célebre director de orquesta ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"2bc36959-dcce-4e10-9ecf-2cd418eaa489","displayText":"Wilfrido Vargas","occurrenceId":"76d17599-5b66-49cd-a49b-8472f1625285"}},{"text":", quien detectó su potencial como vocalista y músico de primera línea, invitándolo a formar parte de su institución musical.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Carrera musical","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"Su etapa junto a ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"2bc36959-dcce-4e10-9ecf-2cd418eaa489","displayText":"Wilfrido Vargas","occurrenceId":"acf4483e-75ce-4e73-81aa-b8b99d8b53cb"}},{"text":" consolidó su estatus, popularizando clásicos del merengue y la salsa que marcaron época, tales como Charo, charo, Cachita, Enrique Blanco y Sansón batalla, una pieza que alcanzó los primeros lugares de popularidad en los mercados caribeños. Posteriormente, fundó su propia agrupación musical llamada Panela, expandiendo su impacto en las pistas de baile y obteniendo reconocimiento en plazas internacionales.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Sin embargo, su fase más fructífera la vivió desde el estudio de grabación como productor y arreglista principal de toda una generación de artistas. Fue el arquitecto sonoro detrás del catálogo de éxitos del grupo de pop urbano ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"1cd11a22-573a-43b4-8f54-fbd08329a4e2","displayText":"Ilegales","occurrenceId":"c60f7026-2691-4e19-a225-881877d3aa95"}},{"text":", diseñó arreglos para figuras de la talla de ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"bc310977-31a9-41bb-9af2-7d3a0d7fabdd","displayText":"Fernando Villalona","occurrenceId":"d6ad201e-c991-47ad-be22-cf81b5c45ad6"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3422883e-7048-48af-bb03-c68c8c557ee4","displayText":"Los Hermanos Rosario","occurrenceId":"0c821034-cd64-4469-8f94-59ee32b8a7f9"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"ae3c0afb-0e0a-4506-bbe6-a59c3c68bb1e","displayText":"Eddy Herrera","occurrenceId":"147102f6-1ff8-475a-a2d3-0b173c3d9d1e"}},{"text":" y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3f8bafec-e5ee-415d-8405-9551cceeeb9b","displayText":"Johnny Ventura","occurrenceId":"a2a67cb6-c57a-44a5-bfc4-609fe2c2f7d0"}},{"text":", y lideró el resurgimiento masivo de la salsa producida en República Dominicana al apadrinar, arreglar y producir los grandes éxitos de intérpretes contemporáneos como ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"14777546-5b72-4dcf-b18a-15320124a0ee","displayText":"Alex Matos","occurrenceId":"ee1d7a5b-9a15-4c61-ab7a-dcff5496a529"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"969a3c82-6b31-4ea0-878d-8643001ca839","displayText":"Yanfourd","occurrenceId":"dedb93e0-a9e7-4a61-a956-b8bf2c7ff7c2"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"69ca4e3b-2a3a-4e61-a5bd-2210606c5f13","displayText":"David Kada","occurrenceId":"3eeb9a2e-9db1-4703-bd36-aa6e80679de7"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"d9ef5d29-573a-4812-b717-18a783d95a70","displayText":"Michel el Buenón","occurrenceId":"6dbaeadb-524d-40c6-afa0-8f110cba4e3d"}},{"text":" y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"33a84471-9f35-4a4f-9b56-b6a61e13212a","displayText":"Revolución Salsera","occurrenceId":"a348ba46-4101-406b-92f3-ebc29cf1e9c2"}},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Estilo musical e impacto cultural","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"El estilo de Víctor Waill se caracterizó por su capacidad para fusionar la sofisticación armónica con la fuerza rítmica del Caribe. En el ámbito de la salsa dominicana, su sello distintivo consistió en introducir secciones de metales agresivas y modernas combinadas con letras de profundo corte romántico, otorgándole una identidad única al género que competía con las potencias tradicionales de la región. Su versatilidad le permitía saltar del merengue bailable a la balada con absoluta naturalidad, ganándose el respeto unánime de sus colegas por su rigurosidad técnica en las consolas de mezcla y su facilidad para identificar el potencial comercial de una canción.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Legado","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"Víctor Waill es recordado como una de las mentes más brillantes de la industria musical caribeña, caracterizado por impulsar constantemente el talento de los nuevos intérpretes. Su fallecimiento ocurrió en su residencia de Santo Domingo a causa de problemas de salud, dejando al país en un profundo estado de conmoción. Su partida física dejó un vacío en la escena tropical, pero su obra permanece intacta a través de los cientos de arreglos musicales que siguen sonando diariamente en las emisoras internacionales y en el estándar de calidad que estableció para la salsa contemporánea.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'ad1e9a2d-9335-4239-8d32-9314ba9739b9';

-- yailin-la-mas-viral [en]: 1
--   "Melymel" -> "MelyMel"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Yailin La Más Viral is a Dominican dembow artist, born Jorgina Guillermo Díaz in Santo Domingo Este on 4 July 2002. She began as a dancer, signed her first deal in 2019, and is now on Roc Nation.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Dancing first","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The order matters. She came into the music from the floor rather than from a studio, and it shows in what she makes: records built for a body to move to, with the hook placed where a dancer would want it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Akino Mundial Music signed her in 2019. The first release came in August 2020 — a remix of Quién Me Atraca a Mí with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"2993cde1-f93d-4cf0-9668-fa1e54b09919","displayText":"Haraca Kiko","occurrenceId":"5f2b8a71-e27e-4fe5-b9bf-4fad61026e26"}},{"text":" — and in December she was on Yo No Me Voy a Acostar with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3e1718be-c12d-42f5-85e7-2156d9574940","displayText":"Tokischa","occurrenceId":"30291e82-302c-4e91-bff9-df28e8261f7a"}},{"text":" and La Perversa. That is the company she started in: the women who had just made Dominican dembow a place where a woman could stand at the front.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Nataly followed, with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"741eb4c0-4ab8-4ad5-8a64-2f156da6a395","displayText":"Ceky Viciny","occurrenceId":"a99a7bcd-7be0-4041-abc5-4e731278c06b"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"580293c3-1e1e-418f-8555-8082665c22b0","displayText":"MelyMel","occurrenceId":"c8b1f716-69a4-496c-8832-0d573e09af37"}},{"text":", La Perversa and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"b3841446-0bdb-48f5-9ace-b492db7d9be2","displayText":"Shadow Blow","occurrenceId":"9ac7feea-33c2-48c0-b314-da107a96e99a"}},{"text":", and it reached number fourteen in the Dominican Republic.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Chivirika","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 2021, Chivirika with El Villano RD went off on TikTok and gave her the name she has carried since. La Más Viral is not modesty; it is an accurate description of the mechanism that made her, and she took it as a title rather than waiting for anyone to grant her one.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Her audience took a name too. They call themselves Team Chivirikas, after that song.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Out of the country","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"By April 2022 she was touring the United States. Si Tú Me Busca, made with the Puerto Rican Anuel AA and produced by Súbelo Neo, reached number twelve on the American Latin chart and ninety-seven in Spain; La Máquina reached twenty-three and seventy-nine; Del Kilo went to twenty at home; Soy Mama came as a remix with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"d08f2c85-7f47-449e-a486-a19ba3b5841a","displayText":"La Insuperable","occurrenceId":"88f7d517-f9bb-4687-9a01-a4c6c418ace7"}},{"text":" and the Colombian Farina.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Solo Tú y Yo with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"b3841446-0bdb-48f5-9ace-b492db7d9be2","displayText":"Shadow Blow","occurrenceId":"3caef4bb-2f79-415b-92ba-004012d213fd"}},{"text":" reached fifteen in the Dominican Republic and took her to a Premio Soberano nomination. Pa Ti and Shaka Laka, both made with the American 6ix9ine, put her on the English-language market, which the Dominican press noted as the point at which a dembowsera stopped being a local proposition.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Resiliencia","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Her own record arrived on 3 April 2024 on Iris Live Music, after the singles Nota and Me Encantas. DM, 69, Sola, Amigos.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"That summer she released Mal de Amor, her first merengue — a Dominican artist of her generation reaching back for the country’s own rhythm rather than outward for somebody else’s, which is a choice more of them are making. Te La Parto Remix with La Más Doll, Ni Un Ki Ki with Jey One, Perdí Mi Tiempo, Step Up with the New York rapper Lil Tjay and Dale 2 with Kreizy K, sixteen at home, came in the same stretch.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Bing Bong","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The run with ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"349e25fa-268a-4036-870f-147e82155f3c","displayText":"Puyalo Pantera","occurrenceId":"79b05a49-cfc7-4d6c-89bb-3acbdc1ed6bf"}},{"text":" is the strongest work of her career. Chapa came first, then Bing Bong, then Silla. Bing Bong reached number eight in the Dominican Republic and twenty-eight on the American Latin chart, and it did what almost no Dominican dembow record manages: it was heard by people who do not otherwise listen to dembow.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"It is built on two syllables that mean nothing at all. That is the craft of the genre stated at its plainest — a sound that lodges before the listener has decided anything about it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"What she has won","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Premios Juventud nominated her in 2023 and Premios Soberano in 2024. In 2025 she took Urbano Dominicano and Fandom del Año at the Premios Heat, and Artista Dembow and Canción Dembow at the Premios Tu Música Urbano, the second of those for Bing Bong. Bing Bong was nominated again at the Premio Lo Nuestro of 2026.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 2026 she spent a week inside Planeta Alofoke, the Dominican web reality programme, and recorded three songs from the set — which is a fair image of how she works. The camera and the record are the same job.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '9b58d66a-3836-412f-8a0f-16b89bb554d2';

-- yailin-la-mas-viral [es]: 1
--   "Melymel" -> "MelyMel"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Yailin La Más Viral es una dembowsera dominicana, nacida Jorgina Guillermo Díaz en Santo Domingo Este el 4 de julio de 2002. Empezó como bailarina, firmó su primer contrato en 2019 y hoy está en Roc Nation.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Primero bailar","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"El orden importa. Llegó a la música desde la pista y no desde un estudio, y eso se nota en lo que hace: discos armados para que un cuerpo se mueva, con el gancho puesto donde una bailadora lo querría.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Akino Mundial Music la firmó en 2019. El primer lanzamiento fue en agosto de 2020 —un remix de Quién Me Atraca a Mí con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"2993cde1-f93d-4cf0-9668-fa1e54b09919","displayText":"Haraca Kiko","occurrenceId":"631fe0e5-6f34-4d27-828a-924828c808f1"}},{"text":"— y en diciembre estaba en Yo No Me Voy a Acostar con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3e1718be-c12d-42f5-85e7-2156d9574940","displayText":"Tokischa","occurrenceId":"93389ecd-9b1e-48ad-b01d-8c00041a4bff"}},{"text":" y La Perversa. Esa es la compañía en la que empezó: las mujeres que acababan de convertir el dembow dominicano en un sitio donde una mujer podía pararse al frente.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Después vino Nataly, con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"741eb4c0-4ab8-4ad5-8a64-2f156da6a395","displayText":"Ceky Viciny","occurrenceId":"ed289c77-2e6d-45d5-9945-c67cbc41e499"}},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"580293c3-1e1e-418f-8555-8082665c22b0","displayText":"MelyMel","occurrenceId":"b3e8837f-c34f-4fce-b066-392385c8ed99"}},{"text":", La Perversa y ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"b3841446-0bdb-48f5-9ace-b492db7d9be2","displayText":"Shadow Blow","occurrenceId":"499064e0-dff6-460c-aa69-871f2cfe94e9"}},{"text":", que llegó al número catorce en República Dominicana.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Chivirika","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"En 2021, Chivirika con El Villano RD explotó en TikTok y le dio el nombre que carga desde entonces. La Más Viral no es modestia; es una descripción exacta del mecanismo que la hizo, y se lo puso ella misma en vez de esperar a que se lo concedieran.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su público también agarró nombre. Se llaman Team Chivirikas, por esa canción.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Salir del país","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Para abril de 2022 estaba de gira por Estados Unidos. Si Tú Me Busca, hecha con el puertorriqueño Anuel AA y producida por Súbelo Neo, llegó al número doce de la lista latina estadounidense y al noventa y siete en España; La Máquina llegó a veintitrés y a setenta y nueve; Del Kilo llegó a veinte en casa; Soy Mama salió como remix con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"d08f2c85-7f47-449e-a486-a19ba3b5841a","displayText":"La Insuperable","occurrenceId":"3a30e438-9aa9-4133-85a9-1672c4824553"}},{"text":" y la colombiana Farina.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Solo Tú y Yo con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"b3841446-0bdb-48f5-9ace-b492db7d9be2","displayText":"Shadow Blow","occurrenceId":"97c7b377-07f8-4749-9f82-57942d7b9547"}},{"text":" llegó a quince en República Dominicana y la llevó a una nominación al Soberano. Pa Ti y Shaka Laka, las dos con el estadounidense 6ix9ine, la metieron en el mercado anglosajón, que la prensa dominicana señaló como el punto en que una dembowsera dejaba de ser una propuesta local.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Resiliencia","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su propio disco salió el 3 de abril de 2024 por Iris Live Music, después de los sencillos Nota y Me Encantas. DM, 69, Sola, Amigos.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Ese verano sacó Mal de Amor, su primer merengue: una artista dominicana de su generación volviendo a buscar el ritmo del país en vez de salir a buscar el de otro, que es una decisión que están tomando cada vez más de ellos. Te La Parto Remix con La Más Doll, Ni Un Ki Ki con Jey One, Perdí Mi Tiempo, Step Up con el rapero neoyorquino Lil Tjay y Dale 2 con Kreizy K, dieciséis en casa, salieron en el mismo tramo.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Bing Bong","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"La tanda con ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"349e25fa-268a-4036-870f-147e82155f3c","displayText":"Puyalo Pantera","occurrenceId":"c6ff9a23-057c-4be4-9b1e-e0280ed02222"}},{"text":" es lo más fuerte de su carrera. Primero Chapa, después Bing Bong, después Silla. Bing Bong llegó al número ocho en República Dominicana y al veintiocho en la lista latina estadounidense, e hizo lo que casi ningún disco de dembow dominicano consigue: lo oyó gente que por lo demás no oye dembow.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Está montada sobre dos sílabas que no significan absolutamente nada. Ese es el oficio del género dicho de la manera más llana: un sonido que se aloja antes de que el oyente haya decidido nada sobre él.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Lo que ha ganado","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Premios Juventud la nominó en 2023 y Premios Soberano en 2024. En 2025 se llevó Urbano Dominicano y Fandom del Año en los Premios Heat, y Artista Dembow y Canción Dembow en los Premios Tu Música Urbano, esta última por Bing Bong. Bing Bong volvió a ser nominada en el Premio Lo Nuestro de 2026.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"En 2026 pasó una semana dentro de Planeta Alofoke, el programa dominicano de telerrealidad web, y grabó tres temas desde el set, que es una imagen justa de cómo trabaja. La cámara y el disco son el mismo oficio.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '0b93afa3-445d-4b70-aa26-2a9b9f1bfe3d';

-- zeo-munoz [en]: 3
--   "Sergio Vargas  " -> "Sergio Vargas"
--   "Víctor Víctor  " -> "Víctor Víctor"
--   "Fernando Villalona  " -> "Fernando Villalona"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zeo Muñoz (born in Santiago, Dominican Republic) is a Dominican singer-songwriter recognized for his introspective lyrics, acoustic sound, and emotionally driven storytelling. Raised in the municipality of Navarrete, Muñoz has become one of the most distinctive voices in the modern Dominican cantautor movement. His work blends folk, pop, and Dominican melodic traditions, earning him recognition across the Dominican Republic, the diaspora, and the New York music scene. He has been nominated for the Premios Soberano and has collaborated with several major Dominican artists.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Early Life and Background","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zeo Muñoz was born in Santiago and taken to Navarrete at three days old, where he was raised by his grandparents. Although Navarrete is officially part of the province of Santiago, Muñoz identifies strongly with the town and often refers to himself as “from Navarrete.”","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His early interest was not singing but writing. Music entered his life through local cultural groups, including a rock band formed by friends and an Andean music ensemble led by cultural promoter Cuki Guzmán. His first instrument was a Peruvian flute, followed later by the guitar.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Muñoz purchased his first guitar by saving small remittances sent by his mother, who had emigrated to the United States through the Panama–Mexico route in the early 1990s. He saved “peso by peso” until he reached the equivalent of 100 dollars, which he used to buy the instrument that would eventually define his artistic path.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Migration to the United States","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"At age 17, Muñoz moved to New York City. He enrolled in university and studied International Criminology with a focus on Government, while working various jobs including lifeguard, swimming instructor, and real estate agent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"During this period, music remained a private hobby. He played guitar at home, wrote songs, and spent time in local parks playing chess and tennis. His early years in New York were marked by loneliness and uncertainty, experiences that later shaped his songwriting.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Musical Beginnings","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Muñoz’s first public musical experience occurred in a university rock band, where he initially served as a songwriter and guitarist. When the lead singer left the group, Muñoz temporarily took over vocals to maintain rehearsals. This was the first time he discovered the emotional connection of singing his own lyrics.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His professional transition began after experiencing symptoms of depression. A psychologist advised him to pursue one of his hobbies more consistently. Muñoz chose music.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He approached a small bar on 184th Street in Manhattan and asked to sing “a couple of songs.” The owner invited him to perform on a Tuesday night. His early audiences were small, but the performances gradually grew, leading him to abandon real estate and dedicate himself fully to music.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Rise in the New York Scene","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zeo Muñoz became a regular performer in bohemian venues across Manhattan and Queens, building a loyal following among Dominican and Latin American audiences. He recorded homemade demos, burned CDs, and distributed them to listeners. His song “Millones de Gallos Rojos” became a turning point, spreading organically through voice notes and social media.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He credits the Dominican community in New York for encouraging him to pursue music professionally.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Collaboration with ","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"7b9ee34b-4438-4032-b827-0b748086e223","displayText":"Wason Brazobán","occurrenceId":"7e9aad65-939a-46de-9c07-002459e5ec4f"}}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"A major milestone in Muñoz’s career occurred when Wason Brazobán, one of the Dominican Republic’s most prominent singer-songwriters, attended one of his performances. Brazobán joined him on stage and later contacted Muñoz to praise “Millones de Gallos Rojos,” predicting it would be a hit.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Brazobán recorded vocals for the song in Muñoz’s living room before heading to the airport. He advised Muñoz to release the solo version first so that his own fame would not overshadow the emerging artist. Muñoz frequently refers to Brazobán as his “musical godfather.”","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Solo Career and Albums","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Muñoz released his debut album “No se lo digan a ella”, which he produced largely at home using digital recording tools. His second album, “Amuleto”, showcased greater rhythmic diversity, including bachata, bolero, and acoustic ballads. Both albums received positive reception for their emotional depth and production quality.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His songwriting style is characterized by poetic imagery, vulnerability, and themes of heartbreak, nostalgia, and personal growth.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Notable Works and Collaborations","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zeo Muñoz has collaborated with several major Dominican artists, including:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"7b9ee34b-4438-4032-b827-0b748086e223","displayText":"Wason Brazobán","occurrenceId":"727c5af5-04c0-4cd3-8ab3-f52420d3d7b9"}}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"059a9e99-5d11-433e-97b9-9c35e57908f1","displayText":"Sergio Vargas","occurrenceId":"3f1d4226-01c5-446f-a3d3-abdcc377cfe3"}}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"4b4bf9da-fe4a-4fc6-be40-1b1b5413dfb3","displayText":"Víctor Víctor","occurrenceId":"72d8e2ee-e1a3-4bd5-b3f0-fe44cac59ea8"}}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"bc310977-31a9-41bb-9af2-7d3a0d7fabdd","displayText":"Fernando Villalona","occurrenceId":"f5cae579-118d-49e0-a7fb-2190476813fd"}}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Franklin Brito  ","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Ronnie Cruz","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His song “Difícil Entender” was inspired by a dream in which he wrote a song for ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"059a9e99-5d11-433e-97b9-9c35e57908f1","displayText":"Sergio Vargas","occurrenceId":"69a9fc92-57cf-4f4c-b666-e78f9672d6cf"}},{"text":". The track later became one of his most acclaimed compositions.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Musical Style","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zeo Muñoz’s music blends:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Acoustic guitar  ","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Folk and cantautor traditions  ","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Dominican melodic sensibilities  ","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Poetic and introspective lyrics  ","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Minimalist arrangements with emotional focus  ","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His voice and writing have been described as intimate, honest, and deeply personal.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Recognition","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Muñoz has been nominated for the Premios Soberano, the Dominican Republic’s highest artistic honor. He has also gained significant support from the Dominican diaspora, particularly in New York, Queens, and the Cibao region.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Legacy and Impact","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Although still early in his career, Zeo Muñoz has become one of the most promising Dominican singer-songwriters of his generation. His organic rise from small bars to national recognition, combined with his authenticity and emotional storytelling, has positioned him as a leading figure in the modern Dominican cantautor movement.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '04d653bc-be32-4cf5-8ddf-f231fda87bcf';

-- zeo-munoz [es]: 4
--   "Wason Brazobán  " -> "Wason Brazobán"
--   "Sergio Vargas  " -> "Sergio Vargas"
--   "Víctor Víctor  " -> "Víctor Víctor"
--   "Fernando Villalona  " -> "Fernando Villalona"
UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zeo Muñoz (nacido en Santiago, República Dominicana) es un cantautor dominicano reconocido por sus letras introspectivas, su sonido acústico y su estilo narrativo profundamente emocional. Criado en el municipio de Navarrete, Muñoz se ha convertido en una de las voces más destacadas del movimiento de cantautores dominicanos contemporáneos. Su música combina elementos del folk, la balada, la canción de autor y la sensibilidad melódica dominicana. Ha sido nominado a los Premios Soberano y ha colaborado con figuras importantes de la música dominicana.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Primeros años","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zeo Muñoz nació en Santiago, pero a los tres días fue llevado a Navarrete, donde creció bajo el cuidado de sus abuelos. Aunque Navarrete pertenece administrativamente a la provincia de Santiago, Muñoz se identifica fuertemente con el municipio y suele presentarse como “de Navarrete”.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su interés inicial no fue el canto, sino la escritura. La música llegó a su vida a través de grupos culturales locales, incluyendo una banda de rock formada por amigos y un conjunto de música andina dirigido por el gestor cultural Cuki Guzmán. Su primer instrumento fue una flauta peruana, y posteriormente la guitarra.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Muñoz adquirió su primera guitarra ahorrando pequeñas remesas enviadas por su madre, quien había emigrado a Estados Unidos en los años 90 atravesando la ruta Panamá–México. Con el equivalente a 100 dólares compró el instrumento que marcaría su futuro artístico.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Migración a Estados Unidos","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"A los 17 años, Muñoz se trasladó a Nueva York. Allí ingresó a la universidad y estudió Criminología Internacional con enfoque en Gobierno, mientras trabajaba como salvavidas, profesor de natación y agente de bienes raíces.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Durante este período, la música era solo un pasatiempo privado. Tocaba guitarra en casa, escribía canciones y pasaba tiempo jugando ajedrez y tenis en parques de Manhattan. La soledad y la incertidumbre de sus primeros años en Estados Unidos influyeron posteriormente en su estilo lírico.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Inicios musicales","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su primera experiencia musical pública ocurrió en una banda universitaria de rock, donde inicialmente era guitarrista y compositor. Cuando el vocalista abandonó el grupo, Muñoz asumió temporalmente el rol de cantante para mantener los ensayos. Fue entonces cuando descubrió la conexión emocional de interpretar sus propias letras.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su transición profesional comenzó tras experimentar síntomas de depresión. Una psicóloga le recomendó dedicarse con mayor frecuencia a uno de sus pasatiempos. Muñoz eligió la música.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Se presentó por primera vez en un pequeño bar de la calle 184 en Manhattan. Las primeras noches apenas reunían a unas pocas personas, pero con el tiempo su presencia en la escena bohemia de Nueva York creció, llevándolo a abandonar los bienes raíces y dedicarse por completo a la música.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Ascenso en la escena de Nueva York","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zeo Muñoz se convirtió en un intérprete habitual en bares y espacios bohemios de Manhattan y Queens. Grababa maquetas caseras, quemaba CDs y los regalaba al público. Su canción “Millones de Gallos Rojos” se convirtió en un punto de inflexión, difundida de manera orgánica a través de notas de voz y redes sociales.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Muñoz reconoce que la comunidad dominicana en Nueva York fue decisiva para que se convenciera de seguir una carrera musical.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Colaboración con Wason Brazobán","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Un momento clave en su trayectoria ocurrió cuando Wason Brazobán asistió a una de sus presentaciones. Brazobán subió al escenario a cantar con él y posteriormente le envió un mensaje elogiando “Millones de Gallos Rojos”, asegurando que sería un éxito.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Brazobán grabó su voz para la canción en la sala de la casa de Muñoz antes de partir hacia el aeropuerto. Le aconsejó lanzar primero la versión en solitario para evitar que su propia fama eclipsara la identidad artística de Muñoz. Desde entonces, Zeo lo considera su “padrino musical”.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Carrera como solista y álbumes","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Muñoz lanzó su primer álbum, “No se lo digan a ella”, producido en gran parte desde su casa utilizando herramientas digitales. Su segundo álbum, “Amuleto”, mostró una evolución rítmica con incursiones en la bachata, el bolero y la balada acústica. Ambos trabajos fueron bien recibidos por su profundidad emocional y su calidad sonora.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su estilo se caracteriza por imágenes poéticas, vulnerabilidad y temas de desamor, nostalgia y crecimiento personal.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Obras destacadas y colaboraciones","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zeo Muñoz ha colaborado con varios artistas dominicanos de renombre, entre ellos:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"7b9ee34b-4438-4032-b827-0b748086e223","displayText":"Wason Brazobán","occurrenceId":"26e37445-b258-4229-8c0c-cd93e4f735bf"}}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"059a9e99-5d11-433e-97b9-9c35e57908f1","displayText":"Sergio Vargas","occurrenceId":"69527677-9250-4fbb-bdc9-69643b7f5f63"}}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"4b4bf9da-fe4a-4fc6-be40-1b1b5413dfb3","displayText":"Víctor Víctor","occurrenceId":"671a266c-f125-4e91-8dc3-51938cea5038"}}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"bc310977-31a9-41bb-9af2-7d3a0d7fabdd","displayText":"Fernando Villalona","occurrenceId":"902e79fe-db3d-4a90-8a96-efc9b4afbb30"}}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Franklin Brito  ","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Ronnie Cruz","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su canción “Difícil Entender” nació de un sueño en el que escribía un tema para ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"059a9e99-5d11-433e-97b9-9c35e57908f1","displayText":"Sergio Vargas","occurrenceId":"242940ce-6697-4dd3-ba1d-337cc2b8eef4"}},{"text":". La pieza se convirtió en una de sus composiciones más reconocidas.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Estilo musical","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"La música de Zeo Muñoz combina:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Guitarra acústica  ","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Tradición de cantautor  ","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Sensibilidad melódica dominicana  ","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Letras introspectivas y poéticas  ","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Arreglos minimalistas centrados en la emoción  ","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su voz y su escritura han sido descritas como íntimas, honestas y profundamente personales.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Reconocimientos","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Zeo Muñoz ha sido nominado a los Premios Soberano, el máximo galardón artístico de la República Dominicana. También ha recibido un fuerte respaldo de la diáspora dominicana, especialmente en Nueva York, Queens y el Cibao.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Legado e impacto","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Aunque su carrera aún está en desarrollo, Zeo Muñoz se ha consolidado como una de las promesas más sólidas del cantautor dominicano contemporáneo. Su ascenso orgánico desde pequeños bares hasta el reconocimiento nacional, junto con su autenticidad y su narrativa emocional, lo posicionan como una figura influyente en la nueva generación de artistas dominicanos.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb WHERE id = '68a51f4d-e669-4a89-b518-3f0eead1f8ad';

COMMIT;
